1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Bengaluru processes 2.5 million quick-commerce
deliveries every single day.

2
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:13,000
Blinkit, Zepto, and Swiggy have solved the warehouse end —
their dark stores have loading docks, their riders have routing.

3
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:18,500
But there is one piece of the delivery journey
nobody has solved: the last 10 metres.

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:24,500
The 2-minute stop at the customer's residential gate —
on a 30-foot bylane with zero legal loading infrastructure.

5
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:30,000
Riders double-park across private gates, blocking residents
and racking up ₹1,500 a month in wrongful police fines.

6
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:38,500
OoruShare fills exactly that gap — turning idle residential driveways
into a cooperative network of authorized 2-minute loading bays,
coordinated by an autonomous agentic curb governor.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,500
OoruShare coordinates four civic stakeholders:
residents who monetize idle driveways,

8
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:52,500
dark stores whose dispatch engine pre-allocates bays
before riders even leave the store,

9
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:58,000
RWA admins who govern access, and riders who arrive
at a legal stop — without installing anything new.

10
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:04,500
Experienced riders already know their local spots.
What they can't solve alone is the 7 PM surge —

11
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:09,500
six riders from the same dark store
flooding the same bylane simultaneously.

12
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:15,500
That's the coordination problem OoruShare fixes at the dispatch layer,
using IBM Granite 3.8B time-series forecasting.

13
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:20,500
The resident dashboard auto-loads with Priya Sharma's driveway data —
one approved spot on 27th Main, HSR Layout.

14
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:25,500
She has earned credits from multiple platform drops —
Blinkit, Zepto, Swiggy, Dunzo.

15
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:30,000
Each drop earns 10 community credits,
redeemable as ₹50 off her monthly society maintenance bill.

16
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:36,000
The rider spatial radar is the fallback interface —
for riders not yet reached by BAA dispatch integration

17
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:41,500
and for new or cross-zone riders
who don't have local street knowledge.

18
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:47,000
The 18 green pulsing pins are RWA-approved,
legally authorized bays across Bengaluru.

19
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:53,000
The three dashed zone circles are live saturation heatmaps —
green: clear, amber: busy, red: throttled.

20
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:56,000
I'll click a pin to book a 2-minute slot.

21
00:01:56,100 --> 00:02:02,500
Dark store managers use this staggering co-pilot.
Instead of flooding a bylane with 6 riders at once,

22
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:08,000
IBM Granite 3.8B recommends dynamic dispatch intervals —
preventing curb bottlenecks before they happen.

23
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:13,500
The live queue shows 14 riders across 4 dark stores:
Blinkit HSR, Zepto Koramangala, Swiggy Indiranagar, Dunzo HSR.

24
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:19,000
I'll hit Simulate Order Surge to add 5 more riders
and watch the agent throttle automatically.

25
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:25,000
RWA administrators retain full democratic governance.
No driveway goes live without committee approval.

26
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:30,500
18 approved, 4 pending review, 2 rejected.
I'll approve a pending spot —

27
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:35,500
— it instantly appears on the rider map.
The credit ledger shows 8 maintenance offsets settled — ₹400 total.

28
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,500
Back on the landing page — the live city telemetry panel
shows real-time zone saturation percentages

29
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:47,000
and the autonomous agent's live perception feed —
the same data powering the rider map heatmap, updated every 8 seconds.

30
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,500
Let me run the full autonomous agent simulation.
Watch as the system perceives an incoming Blinkit dispatch,

31
00:02:55,600 --> 00:03:01,000
reasons about bylane capacity using Granite forecasting,
allocates the optimal bay to a resident,

32
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:06,500
and self-corrects if a conflict is detected.
The entire loop executes in under 500 milliseconds.

33
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Perception → Reasoning → Action → Reflection.
Each phase completing with a verified outcome:

34
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:19,000
4.2 minutes transit saved · 0.14 kg CO₂ abated
· Zero driveway blockage · +10 Credits awarded.

35
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,500
Every architectural decision in OoruShare was made
with IBM Bob as the principal co-architect:

36
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:37,000
the Responsible AI charter written before any code,
the PostgreSQL advisory locking layer,

37
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:42,500
the Redis pub/sub SSE fan-out that scales to 100,000
simultaneous riders at constant database cost,

38
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:47,500
and the full Carbon v11 interface you see here.
The gap OoruShare fills — the last 10 metres —

39
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,500
is one no single quick-commerce platform will build,
because it benefits all of them equally.

40
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:56,000
I'm Pankaj Kumar, SWE AI Full Stack.
This is OoruShare — Built with IBM Bob. Built for Bengaluru.
